---
/**
 * Exists only because it carries trust material a parent actually weighs —
 * founding, who Jayeeta Ma'am is, why small batches, and the approach. Filler
 * would be worse than no page.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ConversionBlock from "../components/ConversionBlock.astro";
import { BUSINESS } from "../data/business.ts";
---

<Base
  title="About Perfect Tuition — Jayeeta Ma'am, Dum Dum Park"
  description="Perfect Tuition was founded in Dum Dum Park in 2012 by Jayeeta Bhattacharya. Small-batch ICSE and CBSE coaching for classes 3–12."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">About Perfect Tuition</h1>

    <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      Perfect Tuition has taught from the same address in Dum Dum Park since {
        BUSINESS.foundedLong
      }. In that time the children who started in class 3 have finished school, and a good number
      of the parents who ring now were sent by parents who came before them.
    </p>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">{BUSINESS.ownerFamiliar}</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        {BUSINESS.owner} founded Perfect Tuition and mentors here. She teaches the senior
        commerce and humanities groups herself, and she is the person who answers the phone when
        you ring — so the conversation about whether this is the right place for your child
        happens with the person who will be teaching them.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        Her approach is unglamorous and it works: know where each child is weak, and go back over
        it until it is no longer weak. Not every student needs the same thing, and a batch small
        enough to notice the difference is the whole point.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">Why the batches are small</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        In a large batch a child who does not understand something has two options: interrupt
        thirty other students, or stay quiet. Most stay quiet, and the gap compounds — by the
        time it shows up in a test, three chapters have been built on top of it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        A small batch removes that choice. The teacher can see who has followed and who has not
        without anyone having to put their hand up, and the child gets the second explanation on
        the day they needed it rather than the week before the exam.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">How we teach</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-ink-soft">
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-brand-600" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span>
            <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Board-specific from the start.</strong> ICSE and
            CBSE ask for different things in the same subject, and a child prepared generically is
            prepared for neither.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-brand-600" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span>
            <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Writing, not just understanding.</strong> Most
            marks are lost between knowing the answer and writing it the way the paper wants it.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-brand-600" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span>
            <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Parents are told the truth.</strong> If a child
            is not working, you will hear it from us before you hear it from the report card.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink">Come and see</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-ink-soft">
        The most useful thing you can do is ring and talk it through — your child's class, board,
        and which subjects are the problem. It takes five minutes and you will know whether this
        is the right place.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <ConversionBlock />
</Base>
